WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.170
Narrator: This is Dumbo, Brooklyn,

00:00:02.170 --> 00:00:05.450
in the opening scene of "Bridge of Spies."

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:09.320
And this is what that same
block actually looks like today.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:13.060
To make this scene
convincingly look like 1957,

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:16.050
every detail in this
street had to be changed,

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:18.630
right down to the font on the signs.

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:21.070
This was all Rena DeAngelo's job,

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:23.100
an Oscar-nominated set decorator

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:26.280
who's worked on period pieces
like "The French Dispatch,"

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:29.420
"West Side Story," "The
Post," and "Mad Men."

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:31.610
Rena: Here's a newsstand
that's in every movie.

00:00:31.610 --> 00:00:32.900
Every single decorator in New York

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:35.070
has probably used it about 10 times.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:38.712
Narrator: Rena took us through
Eclectic/Encore Props in NYC

00:00:38.712 --> 00:00:41.840
to show us how she turned a
modern New York City street

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:44.483
into a 1950s time capsule.

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:46.710
When a location is scouted,

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:49.067
the first order of
business is hiding things

00:00:49.067 --> 00:00:51.120
that don't belong in the era.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.590
That starts with taking
down security cameras

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:55.650
and swapping all the cars in the streets

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:58.800
for vintage ones with
period license plates.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.170
One of the most important details to swap

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:03.450
is any and all signage.

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:07.010
And knowing what signs to put
up means knowing your history.

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:09.350
Rena: They used to paint
advertisements on benches

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:12.040
in the '20s, '30s, '40s, '50s.

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:14.890
These are great. I would put
these in a smoke-shop window.

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:17.530
Narrator: This big tire sign
used in "Bridge of Spies"

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:21.010
helped make Dumbo look properly
industrial for the '50s,

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:22.300
as opposed to its current look

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:24.340
of high-rise apartment buildings.

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:26.790
A lot of neighborhoods were
also historically defined

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:28.010
by their communities,

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:29.826
so you'd need cultural signifiers,

00:01:29.826 --> 00:01:31.142
like these signs in Hebrew

00:01:31.142 --> 00:01:33.150
or specific food advertisements.

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:35.541
Rena: I think I used
Murphy &amp; Ashcom salted fish

00:01:35.541 --> 00:01:36.970
in "Bridge of Spies."

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:39.120
When they're hand-painted,
that makes them look older,

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:40.160
because they haven't been printed.

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:41.490
They're not mass-produced.

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:44.400
Narrator: Small details can
make all the difference.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:45.810
This raised text means

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:48.070
it's probably from the '50s or '60s,

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:49.640
while the flat text on these

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:51.600
means they were produced a bit later,

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.080
since most cities stopped
embossing road signs

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.745
in the '70s and '80s.

00:01:55.745 --> 00:01:57.880
The font is also a giveaway.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:01.100
That spitting sign looks older
than this hair parlor sign

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:02.670
because that typeface has been around

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:04.190
since the 19th century,

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:06.100
whereas this category of sans serifs

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:07.940
was more popular in the '80s.

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:09.690
Besides font and embossing,

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:11.810
the main thing that set decorators look at

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:15.200
when deciding what fits
into their era is materials.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.230
This means knowing what
signs were made from

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:18.573
in previous decades.

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:20.510
Rena: Metal.

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:22.823
Wood.

00:02:22.823 --> 00:02:24.710
Probably from the '20s or '30s,

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:26.650
because it's cast iron.

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:28.273
Narrator: As opposed to the plastics used

00:02:28.273 --> 00:02:29.500
in more recent decades,

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:31.270
which made signs lighter.

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:34.130
Signs, however, can only do so much.

00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:36.720
Some contemporary giveaways are literally

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:38.420
bolted into the ground.

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:41.920
So Rena will cover those using
hollow crates and barrels.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:44.410
Old telephone booths can visually block

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:46.130
other modern features.

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:49.010
This one would fit right in
on a Manhattan street corner

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:51.500
from the mid-'50s to the late '70s.

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:53.750
Rena can tell these pay
phones are from the '50s

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:56.450
based on the width and style of the dial.

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:58.680
The plastic dial and
push button came later,

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:00.490
in the '70s and '80s.

00:03:00.490 --> 00:03:03.910
Strategically placed kiosks
can also hide larger giveaways,

00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:06.430
like modern traffic lights or streetlamps.

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:09.460
She made six of these more
contemporary-looking newsstands

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:11.550
for the '70s setting of "The Post."

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:14.650
For a '50s movie, she'd
take this sort of newsstand

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:17.720
and fill it with magazines,
newspapers, signs,

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:20.320
and products all specific to the decade.

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:23.500
Luckily, Eclectic has a ton of those.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:24.820
Rena: And it's all real packaging,

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:25.740
all this stuff.

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:27.210
Narrator: Besides getting period products

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:28.750
from prop houses like this one,

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:30.940
set decorators will scour the basements

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:32.380
of local grocery stores,

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:34.880
where shopkeepers often
keep surplus products

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:36.180
from decades ago.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:39.100
Rena: Stuff that's good
for a hardware store,

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:40.680
a beauty parlor.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:41.540
Narrator: According to Rena,

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:43.510
the key to making storefronts look real

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:45.630
is the quantity of the products.

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:47.490
She'll sometimes scan this packaging,

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:48.565
print copies of it,

00:03:48.565 --> 00:03:51.990
and use it to wrap rows
and rows of empty boxes.

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:54.040
Set decorators also do a lot of research

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:56.340
into the consumer culture of the era,

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:58.000
especially for a '50s piece,

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:00.150
since consumer goods
had a lot of influence

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:01.560
on the decade's imagery.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:03.890
They need to know what people
were buying and selling

00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:06.910
and what kinds of services
were offered on every block.

00:04:06.910 --> 00:04:08.490
Rena: There was always a shoe-repair shop,

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:10.180
because people didn't
just go buy new shoes

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:11.090
when their shoes wore out.

00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:12.910
They would go and get them fixed.

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:14.690
So on every single street,

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:16.920
there's always a shoe-repair guy.

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:18.240
Narrator: New tech like televisions

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.500
were also often displayed in windows.

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:22.820
Rena: This is perfect
for an electronics store.

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:24.845
Narrator: For the '50s,
you'd want this kind of TV,

00:04:24.845 --> 00:04:25.770
with the rabbit ears,

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:27.980
or any of these other boxy-looking ones.

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:29.250
Rena: This is definitely '50s.

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:30.330
This is definitely '50s.

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:32.690
This is early '50s.

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:34.220
Narrator: She used radios like this

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:36.870
for the '50s setting of "French Dispatch."

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:40.160
Even clothing stores had specific
models for specific eras.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:43.290
Rena: I would put those in
a ladies' clothing store.

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:44.560
Narrator: For the 50 mannequins

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:46.680
that filled the front of
Gimbels department store

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:47.800
in "West Side Story,"

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:49.920
Rena found vintage mannequin heads,

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:52.710
which, for females, were
rounder and had less makeup

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:54.090
compared to later decades.

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:56.140
Rena: See the difference in the faces?

00:04:56.140 --> 00:04:57.610
They just have different structure,

00:04:57.610 --> 00:04:58.770
and the hair is molded.

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:00.930
I would use this in a movie from the '50s.

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:03.560
Narrator: Male mannequins in
the '50s looked much skinnier

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:05.830
and less muscular than they do today,

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:09.730
so she modeled the men's bodies
off teenage-boy mannequins.

00:05:09.730 --> 00:05:12.250
Another element custom-made
for "West Side Story"

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:14.240
was less glamorous.

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:16.165
Rena: So, this is a more contemporary

00:05:16.165 --> 00:05:17.810
city street garbage can,

00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:19.340
which we need to get rid of.

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:22.430
These older wire cans are
what would be on the street

00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:24.360
in the '50s and the '60s.

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:25.840
This one isn't that old,

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:29.550
but we've made it little old
by spraying it down with paint.

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:31.775
On "West Side Story," I
had about 400 of these,

00:05:31.775 --> 00:05:32.900
because everywhere we went,

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:34.850
they wanted garbage everywhere.

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:37.160
Narrator: But some details
are harder to replace,

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:38.700
like lampposts.

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:41.290
Productions could simply replace lampposts

00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:42.560
digitally in post,

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:44.780
but if you want that look on set,

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:46.390
you have to have these wraps.

00:05:46.390 --> 00:05:47.870
Rena: You see these back here?

00:05:47.870 --> 00:05:49.580
Those are fake wraps

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:53.850
so that you can wrap a
contemporary light post

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:55.210
to turn it into one of these

00:05:55.210 --> 00:05:58.140
without bringing one of these
70-million-pound ones out.

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:00.520
Narrator: Designers might
also swap out the light bulbs,

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:01.840
like in "Joker."

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:05.280
Gotham in 1981 would have
had sodium-vapor streetlamps,

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:08.050
which gave off a sort of icky orange glow.

00:06:08.050 --> 00:06:10.650
So the team swapped out
all the modern LED bulbs

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:12.460
for the old sodium-vapor kind

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:14.550
just to get the right lighting.

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:16.660
It's those kinds of fine distinction

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:18.990
that can make or break a period scene.

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:20.210
And with all these details,

00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:22.830
it can take days or even
weeks of street dressing

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:25.840
to film just a few minutes of screen time.

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:27.890
That's what it takes to create a time warp

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:30.836
in the middle of a bustling modern city.

00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:34.760
Rena: We'll make 200 newspapers,

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:36.310
and we'll give props 10

00:06:36.310 --> 00:06:38.820
so that they have them
for people to be reading.

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:40.100
Or if there's 100 people reading,

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:41.480
then props will make 100.

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:42.313
And on "The Post,"

00:06:42.313 --> 00:06:44.753
it was just piles and piles of newspapers.

